-- title:
--   Tightness through closed neighborhoods of compact sets
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real Hausdorff locally convex topological vector space, and let $A\subseteq E$ be convex and von Neumann bounded. Assume the relative topology of $A$ is generated by a countable subfamily of the ambient defining seminorms, and that $A$ is complete and separable in the resulting metrizable additive uniformity. The set $A$ is allowed to be empty. All regularity and convergence below are relative to $A$.
--
--   Write $M(A)$ for the Borel probability measures on $A$ with the weak convergence topology. The barycenter $b(\mu)\in A$ is the Pettis integral of inclusion: every continuous real linear functional $\ell$ is integrable against $\mu$, and $\ell(b(\mu))=\int_A\ell(x)\,\mu(dx)$. The set $A$ is $\mu$-compact when the barycenter preimage of every compact subset of $A$ is compact in $M(A)$; it is pointwise $\mu$-compact when every barycenter fiber is compact.
--
--   Fix a metric $d$ generating the inherited additive uniformity on $A$; this metric is complete. For $K\subseteq A$ and $\delta>0$, write $U_\delta(K)$ for its closed $\delta$-neighborhood, with distance to the empty set interpreted as positive infinity, so $U_\delta(\varnothing)=\varnothing$. Let $M_0\subseteq M(A)$ be arbitrary. Tightness means that for every $\eta>0$ one compact subset of $A$ has complement of mass at most $\eta$ for every measure in $M_0$. Then
--   $$
--   M_0\text{ is tight}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
--   \forall\varepsilon>0\ \forall\delta>0\ \exists K\subseteq A\text{ compact}\ \forall\mu\in M_0,
--   \quad\mu(U_\delta(K))\ge 1-\varepsilon.
--   $$
--   The compact set may depend on both positive real parameters and is chosen before the measure; its closed neighborhood is not required to be compact. There is no upper restriction on $\varepsilon$.
--
--   The criterion tests tightness using neighborhoods of compact sets rather than compact sets themselves.
-- source:
--   Characterization of convex $μ$-compact sets, arXiv:1004.3792v1, PDF p. 5, Lemma 2

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Support50_P33

noncomputable section

open Set Filter MeasureTheory TopologicalSpace

open scoped Topology ENNReal

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P33

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Frontier18.P10

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Dependency50.P25

attribute [local instance] QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P33.paperMeasurable

attribute [local instance] QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P33.paperBorel

section LocallyConvexResults

variable {E : Type*} [UniformSpace E] [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E]
    [hgroup : IsUniformAddGroup E] [hsmul : ContinuousSMul ℝ E]
    [hlc : LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E] [hhaus : T2Space E]

include hgroup hsmul hlc hhaus

/-- Lemma 2, arXiv:1004.3792v1, PDF p.5. The metric d induces the
source's relative additive uniformity, so it is complete on the source
class. Compactness and weak measure topology retain the inherited topology.
Closed thickenings use extended distance, so the empty set has empty
finite-radius thickening. Epsilon is any positive real, also at least one. -/
theorem tightnessNeighborhoodCriterion (A : Set E)
    (hA : GeneralBarycenterSpace A) (hc : Convex ℝ A)
    (d : MetricSpace A) (hd : d.toUniformSpace = (inferInstance : UniformSpace A))
    (M : Set (ProbabilityMeasure A)) :
    IsTightMeasureSet ((fun mu : ProbabilityMeasure A => (mu : Measure A)) '' M) ↔
      ∀ epsilon : ℝ, 0 < epsilon → ∀ delta : ℝ, 0 < delta →
        ∃ K : Set A, IsCompact K ∧
          ∀ mu ∈ M, ENNReal.ofReal (1 - epsilon) ≤
            (mu : Measure A) (letI : MetricSpace A := d; Metric.cthickening delta K) := by sorry

end LocallyConvexResults

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Support50.P33

end
